1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,832
You made great commits on your laptop.

2
00:00:02,832 --> 00:00:03,385
Now what?

3
00:00:03,385 --> 00:00:04,551
If the laptop dies,

4
00:00:04,551 --> 00:00:05,410
they are gone.

5
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:07,497
And your team has never seen them.

6
00:00:08,047 --> 00:00:11,175
Today you connect your repository to a shared copy,

7
00:00:11,175 --> 00:00:14,058
and learn the daily rhythm of team work: fetch,

8
00:00:14,058 --> 00:00:14,365
pull,

9
00:00:14,365 --> 00:00:14,917
and push.

10
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:16,068
Plus tags,

11
00:00:16,068 --> 00:00:19,193
which stamp a version like one point oh on a commit,

12
00:00:19,193 --> 00:00:21,717
so everyone can find exactly what shipped.

13
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:25,083
As always, there is a lab with a checker at the end.

14
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:30,571
A remote is just another copy of your repository,

15
00:00:30,571 --> 00:00:31,974
usually on a server.

16
00:00:31,974 --> 00:00:34,290
Git gives each remote a nickname,

17
00:00:34,290 --> 00:00:36,114
and the default is origin.

18
00:00:36,664 --> 00:00:37,565
When you clone,

19
00:00:37,565 --> 00:00:40,868
Git copies everything and remembers where it came from.

20
00:00:40,868 --> 00:00:42,190
That source is origin.

21
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:48,170
Git also keeps a bookmark of where the remote branch was the last time you talked to it.

22
00:00:48,170 --> 00:00:50,083
It is called origin slash main,

23
00:00:50,083 --> 00:00:51,625
a remote tracking branch.

24
00:00:51,625 --> 00:00:54,217
It only moves when you talk to the server.

25
00:00:54,767 --> 00:00:57,926
Fetch downloads new commits and moves that bookmark.

26
00:00:57,926 --> 00:00:59,870
It never touches your own files,

27
00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:01,146
so it is always safe.

28
00:01:01,696 --> 00:01:02,547
Pull is fetch,

29
00:01:02,547 --> 00:01:04,068
plus a merge or a rebase.

30
00:01:04,068 --> 00:01:05,710
It updates your own branch,

31
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:07,413
so it can change your files.

32
00:01:07,963 --> 00:01:09,743
And push uploads your commits.

33
00:01:09,743 --> 00:01:13,659
Git only allows it when you will not overwrite anyone else's work.

34
00:01:15,709 --> 00:01:17,217
Let us see it live.

35
00:01:17,217 --> 00:01:20,075
A team repository lives on a server,

36
00:01:20,075 --> 00:01:21,266
at slash S R V,

37
00:01:21,266 --> 00:01:22,060
slash git,

38
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:23,568
slash team dot git.

39
00:01:23,568 --> 00:01:24,283
Clone it.

40
00:01:24,283 --> 00:01:25,553
Type: git clone,

41
00:01:25,553 --> 00:01:26,505
slash S R V,

42
00:01:26,505 --> 00:01:27,299
slash git,

43
00:01:27,299 --> 00:01:28,808
slash team dot git.

44
00:01:29,358 --> 00:01:32,472
Cloned. Go into the folder. Type: CD, team.

45
00:01:33,022 --> 00:01:36,094
List the remotes. Type: git remote, dash V.

46
00:01:36,644 --> 00:01:37,105
Origin,

47
00:01:37,105 --> 00:01:40,329
with an address for fetching and one for pushing.

48
00:01:40,329 --> 00:01:41,447
Now the branches,

49
00:01:41,447 --> 00:01:42,763
and what they track.

50
00:01:42,763 --> 00:01:43,882
Type: git branch,

51
00:01:43,882 --> 00:01:44,474
dash V V.

52
00:01:45,024 --> 00:01:47,079
Main tracks origin slash main,

53
00:01:47,079 --> 00:01:48,792
shown in square brackets.

54
00:01:48,792 --> 00:01:50,299
Now list every branch,

55
00:01:50,299 --> 00:01:51,806
including remote ones.

56
00:01:51,806 --> 00:01:52,971
Type: git branch,

57
00:01:52,971 --> 00:01:53,450
dash A.

58
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,554
The remote tracking branches live under remotes slash origin.

59
00:01:57,554 --> 00:01:59,011
You never commit on them.

60
00:01:59,011 --> 00:02:00,934
They are bookmarks of the server.

61
00:02:02,984 --> 00:02:08,147
Now a teammate pushes while you are working. Go into your clone. Type: CD, team.

62
00:02:08,697 --> 00:02:11,299
Ask for the status. Type: git status.

63
00:02:11,849 --> 00:02:14,211
Up to date with origin slash main.

64
00:02:14,211 --> 00:02:18,657
But careful: that only means up to date with your last bookmark.

65
00:02:18,657 --> 00:02:20,672
Git has not asked the server.

66
00:02:20,672 --> 00:02:21,575
Ask for news.

67
00:02:21,575 --> 00:02:22,687
Type: git fetch.

68
00:02:23,237 --> 00:02:28,250
A new commit arrived on origin slash main. Check again. Type: git status.

69
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:31,298
Now it knows: behind by one commit.

70
00:02:31,298 --> 00:02:32,725
See what is waiting,

71
00:02:32,725 --> 00:02:34,081
before you take it.

72
00:02:34,081 --> 00:02:35,080
Type: git log,

73
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:36,436
dash dash one line,

74
00:02:36,436 --> 00:02:36,793
main,

75
00:02:36,793 --> 00:02:37,435
two dots,

76
00:02:37,435 --> 00:02:38,720
origin slash main.

77
00:02:39,270 --> 00:02:43,116
Two dots mean: commits on the right that are not on the left.

78
00:02:43,116 --> 00:02:43,810
One commit,

79
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:44,441
from Alex.

80
00:02:44,441 --> 00:02:45,449
Now bring it in.

81
00:02:45,449 --> 00:02:46,395
Type: git pull.

82
00:02:46,945 --> 00:02:47,843
Fast forward,

83
00:02:47,843 --> 00:02:49,431
and your files updated.

84
00:02:49,431 --> 00:02:50,811
Look at the history.

85
00:02:50,811 --> 00:02:51,778
Type: git log,

86
00:02:51,778 --> 00:02:53,089
dash dash one line.

87
00:02:53,639 --> 00:02:57,095
A good habit: fetch, look, then pull. Nothing surprises you.

88
00:02:59,145 --> 00:03:02,345
Now share your own work. Go in. Type: CD, team.

89
00:03:02,895 --> 00:03:05,160
Make the start script friendlier.

90
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:05,915
Type: echo,

91
00:03:05,915 --> 00:03:06,327
quote,

92
00:03:06,327 --> 00:03:07,082
echo ready,

93
00:03:07,082 --> 00:03:07,494
quote,

94
00:03:07,494 --> 00:03:08,249
two arrows,

95
00:03:08,249 --> 00:03:09,210
start dot S H.

96
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:14,090
Commit it. Type: git commit, dash A M, quote, Print ready message, quote.

97
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:16,582
Now share it. Type: git push.

98
00:03:17,132 --> 00:03:17,731
Rejected!

99
00:03:17,731 --> 00:03:20,394
The remote has a commit you do not have,

100
00:03:20,394 --> 00:03:22,524
and Git refuses to overwrite it.

101
00:03:22,524 --> 00:03:23,922
That is a protection,

102
00:03:23,922 --> 00:03:24,588
not a bug.

103
00:03:24,588 --> 00:03:26,585
The hint says it: fetch first.

104
00:03:26,585 --> 00:03:28,316
Pull your teammate's work,

105
00:03:28,316 --> 00:03:29,913
and replay yours on top.

106
00:03:29,913 --> 00:03:30,912
Type: git pull,

107
00:03:30,912 --> 00:03:32,044
dash dash rebase.

108
00:03:32,594 --> 00:03:35,346
Rebased. Now push again. Type: git push.

109
00:03:35,896 --> 00:03:36,380
Pushed.

110
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:37,764
Look at the picture.

111
00:03:37,764 --> 00:03:38,733
Type: git log,

112
00:03:38,733 --> 00:03:40,049
dash dash one line,

113
00:03:40,049 --> 00:03:41,156
dash dash graph,

114
00:03:41,156 --> 00:03:42,125
dash dash all.

115
00:03:42,675 --> 00:03:45,967
Origin slash main and your main sit on the same commit,

116
00:03:45,967 --> 00:03:47,104
in a straight line.

117
00:03:47,104 --> 00:03:51,592
And never force push to a shared branch: it can erase your teammates' work.

118
00:03:53,742 --> 00:03:55,022
Last skill: tags.

119
00:03:55,022 --> 00:03:58,108
A tag is a permanent name for one commit,

120
00:03:58,108 --> 00:04:00,066
like version one point oh.

121
00:04:00,066 --> 00:04:00,517
Go in.

122
00:04:00,517 --> 00:04:01,195
Type: CD,

123
00:04:01,195 --> 00:04:01,571
team.

124
00:04:02,121 --> 00:04:03,837
Create an annotated tag,

125
00:04:03,837 --> 00:04:04,910
with a message.

126
00:04:04,910 --> 00:04:05,911
Type: git tag,

127
00:04:05,911 --> 00:04:06,412
dash A,

128
00:04:06,412 --> 00:04:07,484
V one dot zero,

129
00:04:07,484 --> 00:04:07,985
dash M,

130
00:04:07,985 --> 00:04:08,414
quote,

131
00:04:08,414 --> 00:04:09,415
First release,

132
00:04:09,415 --> 00:04:09,844
quote.

133
00:04:10,394 --> 00:04:12,549
List the tags. Type: git tag.

134
00:04:13,099 --> 00:04:18,091
See what the tag holds. Type: git show, dash dash stat, V one dot zero.

135
00:04:18,641 --> 00:04:19,376
The tagger,

136
00:04:19,376 --> 00:04:21,046
the date and the message,

137
00:04:21,046 --> 00:04:22,984
then the commit it points to.

138
00:04:22,984 --> 00:04:25,256
Tags are not pushed automatically.

139
00:04:25,256 --> 00:04:26,259
Type: git push,

140
00:04:26,259 --> 00:04:26,726
origin,

141
00:04:26,726 --> 00:04:27,729
V one dot zero.

142
00:04:28,279 --> 00:04:29,796
Pushed as a new tag.

143
00:04:29,796 --> 00:04:31,464
Confirm on the server.

144
00:04:31,464 --> 00:04:33,437
Type: git L S dash remote,

145
00:04:33,437 --> 00:04:34,574
dash dash tags,

146
00:04:34,574 --> 00:04:35,105
origin.

147
00:04:35,655 --> 00:04:37,358
The tag is on the server.

148
00:04:37,358 --> 00:04:40,354
Everyone can check out exactly this version.

149
00:04:40,354 --> 00:04:42,397
To push all your tags at once,

150
00:04:42,397 --> 00:04:44,781
use git push origin dash dash tags.

151
00:04:44,781 --> 00:04:45,462
On GitHub,

152
00:04:45,462 --> 00:04:47,096
a tag becomes a release,

153
00:04:47,096 --> 00:04:48,798
with notes and downloads,

154
00:04:48,798 --> 00:04:51,250
and we will make one on screen soon.

155
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:56,173
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

156
00:04:56,723 --> 00:04:59,179
The lab is lab sixty-one dot S H,

157
00:04:59,179 --> 00:05:02,974
in the academy slash lab folder of git hub dot com,

158
00:05:02,974 --> 00:05:04,462
slash it dash waves,

159
00:05:04,462 --> 00:05:06,173
slash dev ops dash A I.

160
00:05:06,173 --> 00:05:07,885
Install it like before,

161
00:05:07,885 --> 00:05:09,075
then run lab up.

162
00:05:09,625 --> 00:05:11,284
It builds a shared repository,

163
00:05:11,284 --> 00:05:12,445
and your clone of it.

164
00:05:12,445 --> 00:05:13,551
While you were away,

165
00:05:13,551 --> 00:05:15,044
a teammate pushed a commit.

166
00:05:15,594 --> 00:05:19,818
One: create release notes dot text with one line of text, and commit it.

167
00:05:20,368 --> 00:05:21,110
Two: push it.

168
00:05:21,110 --> 00:05:22,536
Git will reject the push,

169
00:05:22,536 --> 00:05:24,533
so pull your teammate's work first,

170
00:05:24,533 --> 00:05:25,445
then push again.

171
00:05:25,995 --> 00:05:30,817
Three: create an annotated tag, V one dot zero, and push it to origin.

172
00:05:31,367 --> 00:05:36,423
Four: in answers dot text, write one number: how many commits origin main has now.

173
00:05:36,973 --> 00:05:41,026
Then run lab check. Four pass lines. Fetch first, look, then pull.

174
00:05:43,076 --> 00:05:45,060
Six points, and you need all six.

175
00:05:45,610 --> 00:05:47,445
The checker shows four pass lines.

176
00:05:47,995 --> 00:05:50,619
You pulled before you pushed, and never used force.

177
00:05:51,169 --> 00:05:53,451
The tag is annotated and on the server.

178
00:05:54,001 --> 00:05:56,519
You can explain what fetch does that pull does not.

179
00:05:57,069 --> 00:05:59,394
The number in answers dot text is correct.

180
00:05:59,944 --> 00:06:01,587
You ran lab down to clean up.

181
00:06:02,137 --> 00:06:03,758
To pass, you need all six.

182
00:06:05,808 --> 00:06:08,645
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

183
00:06:09,195 --> 00:06:11,414
One: install the lab, and run lab up.

184
00:06:11,964 --> 00:06:13,585
Two: commit the release notes.

185
00:06:14,135 --> 00:06:17,570
Three: push, fix the rejection by pulling, and push again.

186
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:19,933
Four: tag, and push the tag.

187
00:06:20,483 --> 00:06:23,854
Five: count the commits, write the number, and run lab check.

188
00:06:24,404 --> 00:06:25,876
Six: run lab down.

189
00:06:26,426 --> 00:06:28,261
Pause the video, and begin now.

190
00:06:43,811 --> 00:06:44,686
Quick check.

191
00:06:45,236 --> 00:06:47,049
What does fetch do that pull does not?

192
00:06:47,599 --> 00:06:49,220
What is origin slash main?

193
00:06:49,770 --> 00:06:51,584
Why was your first push rejected?

194
00:06:52,134 --> 00:06:53,904
Are tags pushed automatically?

195
00:06:54,454 --> 00:07:00,726
Objective covered: M C zero seven, objective five, remotes, fetch, pull, push, and tags.

196
00:07:01,276 --> 00:07:03,346
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

197
00:07:13,896 --> 00:07:17,352
Next: pull requests, reviews, and protected branches.

198
00:07:17,902 --> 00:07:21,230
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
